1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
Welcome! In this video, we'll build a complete REST API
from scratch using Node.js and Express.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:06,000
By the end, you'll understand how to set up an Express server,
create API endpoints, and deploy your API.

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:09,000
Let's get started! First, make sure you have Node.js 16 or higher
installed on your computer.

4
00:00:09,500 --> 00:00:12,000
Open your terminal and check your Node.js version
with the command: node --version

5
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:15,000
Great! Now let's create our project directory.
Type: mkdir my-rest-api

6
00:00:15,500 --> 00:00:18,000
Then navigate into it: cd my-rest-api

7
00:00:18,500 --> 00:00:21,000
Initialize npm in the project: npm init -y

8
00:00:21,500 --> 00:00:24,000
Now let's install Express, which is the framework
we'll use to build our API.

9
00:00:24,500 --> 00:00:27,000
Type: npm install express cors

10
00:00:27,500 --> 00:00:30,000
We're also installing CORS to handle cross-origin requests.
This installation takes about 10 seconds.

11
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:33,500
Perfect! Now let's create our server file.
Create a file called server.js

12
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:36,500
And open it in your code editor.

13
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:40,000
Now, let's write the code to create an Express server.
Type the following code:

14
00:00:40,500 --> 00:00:44,000
const express = require('express');
const cors = require('cors');

15
00:00:44,500 --> 00:00:47,000
const app = express();
const PORT = 3000;

16
00:00:47,500 --> 00:00:50,000
Next, we add middleware:
app.use(cors());
app.use(express.json());

17
00:00:50,500 --> 00:00:53,000
Finally, we start the server:
app.listen(PORT, () => {

18
00:00:53,500 --> 00:00:56,000
  console.log(`Server running on port ${PORT}`);
});

19
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Here's what's happening:
1. We import Express and CORS libraries
2. We create an app instance
3. We add middleware to handle JSON and cross-origin requests

20
00:01:00,500 --> 00:01:03,000
4. We start the server on port 3000

21
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:07,000
Now let's add our first API endpoint.
Add this code before app.listen():

22
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:10,000
app.get('/', (req, res) => {
  res.json({ message: 'Welcome to our API' });
});

23
00:01:10,500 --> 00:01:13,000
This endpoint:
- Uses the GET HTTP method
- Responds to requests to the root path '/'

24
00:01:13,500 --> 00:01:16,000
- Returns a JSON response with a welcome message

25
00:01:16,500 --> 00:01:19,000
Let's test it. In the terminal, type:
npm start

26
00:01:19,500 --> 00:01:22,000
Or if you installed nodemon for auto-reload:
nodemon server.js

27
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:25,000
Great! The server is running on port 3000.
Now let's test the endpoint using Postman.

28
00:01:25,500 --> 00:01:28,000
Create a GET request to http://localhost:3000/

29
00:01:28,500 --> 00:01:31,000
Click Send.

30
00:01:31,500 --> 00:01:34,000
Excellent! Our first endpoint is working.
We got the welcome message in JSON format.

31
00:01:34,500 --> 00:01:37,000
Now let's create more endpoints to make this a real API.

32
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:40,500
Let's create a simple in-memory database with some users.
Add this code before your routes:

33
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:44,000
let users = [
  { id: 1, name: 'Alice', email: 'alice@example.com' },

34
00:01:44,500 --> 00:01:47,000
  { id: 2, name: 'Bob', email: 'bob@example.com' }
];

35
00:01:47,500 --> 00:01:50,000
Now let's add our first user endpoint.
This will GET all users:

36
00:01:50,500 --> 00:01:53,000
app.get('/api/users', (req, res) => {
  res.json(users);
});

37
00:01:53,500 --> 00:01:56,000
Let's add another endpoint to get a specific user by ID:

38
00:01:56,500 --> 00:01:59,000
app.get('/api/users/:id', (req, res) => {
  const user = users.find(u =>

39
00:01:59,500 --> 00:02:02,000
    u.id === parseInt(req.params.id)
  );

40
00:02:02,500 --> 00:02:05,000
  if (!user) {
    return res.status(404).json({ error: 'User not found' });
  }
  res.json(user);
});

41
00:02:05,500 --> 00:02:08,000
The `:id` in the route is a parameter.
We access it with req.params.id

42
00:02:08,500 --> 00:02:11,000
Now let's add a POST endpoint to create a new user:

43
00:02:11,500 --> 00:02:14,500
app.post('/api/users', (req, res) => {
  const { name, email } = req.body;

44
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:17,500
  if (!name || !email) {
    return res.status(400).json({

45
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:20,500
      error: 'Name and email required'
    });
  }

46
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:23,500
  const newUser = {
    id: users.length + 1,
    name,

47
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:26,500
    email
  };

48
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:29,500
  users.push(newUser);
  res.status(201).json(newUser);
});

49
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:33,000
Now let's restart the server and test these endpoints.

50
00:02:33,500 --> 00:02:36,000
Press Ctrl+C to stop the server.

51
00:02:36,500 --> 00:02:39,000
Then start it again: npm start

52
00:02:39,500 --> 00:02:42,000
Now let's test the endpoints in Postman.
First, GET all users:

53
00:02:42,500 --> 00:02:45,000
Make a GET request to http://localhost:3000/api/users

54
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:48,000
Click Send.

55
00:02:48,500 --> 00:02:51,000
Great! We got both users in the response.

56
00:02:51,500 --> 00:02:54,000
Now let's get a specific user. Change the URL to:
http://localhost:3000/api/users/1

57
00:02:54,500 --> 00:02:57,000
Click Send.

58
00:02:57,500 --> 00:03:00,000
Perfect! We got Alice's data.

59
00:03:00,500 --> 00:03:03,000
Now let's create a new user using POST.
Change the method to POST

60
00:03:03,500 --> 00:03:06,000
The URL stays: http://localhost:3000/api/users

61
00:03:06,500 --> 00:03:09,000
Click the Body tab, select "raw", and change to JSON.

62
00:03:09,500 --> 00:03:12,000
Type the following JSON:

63
00:03:12,500 --> 00:03:15,000
{
  "name": "Charlie",
  "email": "charlie@example.com"
}

64
00:03:15,500 --> 00:03:18,000
Click Send.

65
00:03:18,500 --> 00:03:21,000
Excellent! We successfully created a new user.

66
00:03:21,500 --> 00:03:24,000
We've successfully:
- Retrieved all users
- Retrieved a specific user
- Created a new user

67
00:03:24,500 --> 00:03:27,000
This is the core of a REST API!

68
00:03:27,500 --> 00:03:30,000
Now let's add proper error handling.
A good API needs to handle errors gracefully.

69
00:03:30,500 --> 00:03:33,000
Add this error handling middleware at the end of your code,
before app.listen():

70
00:03:33,500 --> 00:03:36,000
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  console.error(err);

71
00:03:36,500 --> 00:03:39,000
  res.status(500).json({
    error: 'Internal server error'
  });
});

72
00:03:39,500 --> 00:03:42,000
Also add a 404 handler for non-existent routes:

73
00:03:42,500 --> 00:03:45,000
app.use((req, res) => {
  res.status(404).json({
    error: 'Not found'
  });
});

74
00:03:45,500 --> 00:03:48,000
Restart the server and test it.

75
00:03:48,500 --> 00:03:51,000
Try accessing a non-existent endpoint:
GET http://localhost:3000/api/invalid

76
00:03:51,500 --> 00:03:54,000
Click Send.

77
00:03:54,500 --> 00:03:57,000
Perfect! Our error handling is working.
We get a 404 error with a proper error message.

78
00:03:57,500 --> 00:04:00,000
Now let's add environment variables for the port.
This is important for deployment.

79
00:04:00,500 --> 00:04:03,000
Change this line:
const PORT = 3000;

80
00:04:03,500 --> 00:04:06,000
To:
const PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;

81
00:04:06,500 --> 00:04:09,000
Now we can set the PORT environment variable,
and the app will use that. If not set, it defaults to 3000.

82
00:04:09,500 --> 00:04:12,000
Great! We've built a complete REST API with:
- Express server setup
- GET and POST endpoints
- Error handling
- Environment variables

83
00:04:12,500 --> 00:04:15,000
All in just 15 minutes!

84
00:04:15,500 --> 00:04:18,000
Here are some next steps to improve your API:

85
00:04:18,500 --> 00:04:21,000
1. Database: Use PostgreSQL instead of in-memory arrays

86
00:04:21,500 --> 00:04:24,000
2. Authentication: Add JWT authentication

87
00:04:24,500 --> 00:04:27,000
3. Validation: Use a library like Joi for request validation

88
00:04:27,500 --> 00:04:30,000
4. Documentation: Create API documentation with Swagger

89
00:04:30,500 --> 00:04:33,000
5. Deployment: Deploy to Heroku or AWS

90
00:04:33,500 --> 00:04:36,000
6. Testing: Write automated tests

91
00:04:36,500 --> 00:04:39,000
Check out the GitHub repository for the complete code.
All the links are in the description.

92
00:04:39,500 --> 00:04:42,000
Thanks for watching! If you found this helpful:
- Like and subscribe for more tutorials

93
00:04:42,500 --> 00:04:45,000
- Check out the linked repository for the full code

94
00:04:45,500 --> 00:04:48,000
- Let me know what you'd like to see next in the comments

95
00:04:48,500 --> 00:04:51,000
Good luck with your API development!
See you in the next video!
